WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.250 --> 00:00:03.200
- We've all heard that, "If
you can't say something nice,

00:00:03.200 --> 00:00:05.000
don't say anything at all,"

00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:08.320
which was either said by Aesop or Thumper.

00:00:08.320 --> 00:00:09.727
The Book of Proverbs says,

00:00:09.727 --> 00:00:12.090
"A gossip separates close friends."

00:00:12.090 --> 00:00:14.070
In this quote, that's been attributed

00:00:14.070 --> 00:00:17.770
to both Socrates and Eleanor
Roosevelt, both wrong,

00:00:17.770 --> 00:00:20.290
goes, "Great minds discuss ideas.

00:00:20.290 --> 00:00:21.870
Average minds discuss events.

00:00:21.870 --> 00:00:23.690
Small minds discuss people."

00:00:23.690 --> 00:00:25.260
Elitist much?

00:00:25.260 --> 00:00:27.570
Well, I guess we're all small-minded

00:00:27.570 --> 00:00:29.410
because most human conversations

00:00:29.410 --> 00:00:32.150
are not about quantum
physics, or existentialism,

00:00:32.150 --> 00:00:33.770
or Cartesian geometry,

00:00:33.770 --> 00:00:36.030
but the private lives of others:

00:00:36.030 --> 00:00:39.370
from social media to
reality TV, to the tabloids,

00:00:39.370 --> 00:00:41.820
we are obsessed with sharing dirt.

00:00:41.820 --> 00:00:45.400
Even most political coverage
is less concerned with policy

00:00:45.400 --> 00:00:47.500
than it is with alliances, and snubs,

00:00:47.500 --> 00:00:49.210
and who's backstabbing who.

00:00:49.210 --> 00:00:51.630
If you could eavesdrop on
a bunch of astrophysicists

00:00:51.630 --> 00:00:53.020
on their lunch break,

00:00:53.020 --> 00:00:55.760
they're less likely to be
chatting about black holes

00:00:55.760 --> 00:00:57.670
than about how, "So-and-so
is getting tenure

00:00:57.670 --> 00:00:59.050
even though he doesn't deserve it,"

00:00:59.050 --> 00:01:01.177
and that, "You-know-who lost her funding."

00:01:01.177 --> 00:01:02.360
"You don't say!"

00:01:02.360 --> 00:01:04.900
Gossip accounts for so much human speech

00:01:04.900 --> 00:01:08.290
it stands to reason that it
has some evolutionary purpose.

00:01:08.290 --> 00:01:10.810
In fact, some anthropologists believe

00:01:10.810 --> 00:01:13.210
that it's not just an
important part of language,

00:01:13.210 --> 00:01:16.510
but the whole reason language
evolved in the first place.

00:01:16.510 --> 00:01:19.283
I'm Dr. Erica Brozovsky,
and this is "Otherwords!"

00:01:19.283 --> 00:01:21.866
(bright music)

00:01:26.656 --> 00:01:28.739
- [Announcer] Otherwords!

00:01:30.040 --> 00:01:33.890
- Social scientists define
gossip by three main aspects.

00:01:33.890 --> 00:01:37.600
One, it's a conversation about
someone who is not present.

00:01:37.600 --> 00:01:40.960
Two, it involves some
kind of moral judgment.

00:01:40.960 --> 00:01:43.220
And three, it's fun!

00:01:43.220 --> 00:01:46.520
Gossiping is not a
chore, we enjoy doing it.

00:01:46.520 --> 00:01:48.340
And like sex or sugar,

00:01:48.340 --> 00:01:49.480
things that give us pleasure

00:01:49.480 --> 00:01:51.480
tend to have a biological root.

00:01:51.480 --> 00:01:52.700
To find it, we asked our friend,

00:01:52.700 --> 00:01:54.480
Dr. Joe Hanson from "Be Smart"

00:01:54.480 --> 00:01:57.520
to spill the tea about
our ape-like ancestors.

00:01:57.520 --> 00:01:59.910
- Being part of a group is great.

00:01:59.910 --> 00:02:01.700
You get cooperation in finding food,

00:02:01.700 --> 00:02:03.350
protection from predators,

00:02:03.350 --> 00:02:05.150
even get help raising your babies.

00:02:05.150 --> 00:02:06.980
But, there are challenges.

00:02:06.980 --> 00:02:09.600
The bigger the group, the
more resources are needed

00:02:09.600 --> 00:02:13.280
and more internal competition
for those resources.

00:02:13.280 --> 00:02:15.790
Tempers flare, fights break out,

00:02:15.790 --> 00:02:18.107
you got to know who's a
friend and who's a foe.

00:02:18.107 --> 00:02:21.460
And there's the ever-present
problem of freeloaders.

00:02:21.460 --> 00:02:24.260
Those are members who enjoy
the benefits of group living

00:02:24.260 --> 00:02:25.770
without pitching in.

00:02:25.770 --> 00:02:28.450
Social animals protect
themselves from freeloaders

00:02:28.450 --> 00:02:31.230
by keeping track of who's
not pulling their weight.

00:02:31.230 --> 00:02:33.890
Everyone needs to take a
turn at the edge of the herd

00:02:33.890 --> 00:02:35.160
or the front of the flock.

00:02:35.160 --> 00:02:37.160
One of the main ways that primates do this

00:02:37.160 --> 00:02:39.260
is through reciprocal grooming.

00:02:39.260 --> 00:02:42.510
Literally, you scratch my
back and I'll scratch yours,

00:02:42.510 --> 00:02:44.830
and I might pick a few
lice off while I'm at it.

00:02:44.830 --> 00:02:46.700
It's a way to establish hierarchies

00:02:46.700 --> 00:02:49.280
and show that you're
dependable and trustworthy.

00:02:49.280 --> 00:02:51.040
I mean, the monkey that gets groomed

00:02:51.040 --> 00:02:52.350
but doesn't reciprocate,

00:02:52.350 --> 00:02:54.550
yeah, no one's gonna bother to warn him

00:02:54.550 --> 00:02:57.270
when a saber-toothed tiger sneaks up.

00:02:57.270 --> 00:03:01.540
This may be sufficient for
bands of 40 to 50 members.

00:03:01.540 --> 00:03:04.970
But, as early hominids
expanded their group size,

00:03:04.970 --> 00:03:07.540
reciprocal grooming
became too time-consuming.

00:03:07.540 --> 00:03:10.830
I mean, to form social
bonds with 100 individuals,

00:03:10.830 --> 00:03:12.940
you'd have to spend so
much of the day grooming

00:03:12.940 --> 00:03:15.700
that there'd be no time for anything else.

00:03:15.700 --> 00:03:20.640
So, how can you know who's
trustworthy and who's not?

00:03:20.640 --> 00:03:21.480
- This is the problem

00:03:21.480 --> 00:03:23.720
that anthropologist Robin Dunbar believes

00:03:23.720 --> 00:03:25.900
spurred the evolution of language.

00:03:25.900 --> 00:03:28.100
Gossip, he argues, replace grooming

00:03:28.100 --> 00:03:31.960
as the primary way we maintain
social order in large groups.

00:03:31.960 --> 00:03:34.610
You don't need to go through
elaborate grooming rituals

00:03:34.610 --> 00:03:36.610
when you can just hear
through the grapevine

00:03:36.610 --> 00:03:39.940
who's a dependable member
of the tri-band, who's not.

00:03:39.940 --> 00:03:42.730
He argues that the main
reason our brains got bigger

00:03:42.730 --> 00:03:44.080
and more complex

00:03:44.080 --> 00:03:46.140
was not so we could design better tools

00:03:46.140 --> 00:03:47.770
or be better hunters,

00:03:47.770 --> 00:03:51.150
but so we could keep track
of more social relationships.

00:03:51.150 --> 00:03:52.790
He even found a correlation

00:03:52.790 --> 00:03:55.210
between the relative
size of mammal's cortex

00:03:55.210 --> 00:03:56.930
and the size of its social group.

00:03:56.930 --> 00:03:58.510
It's known as Dunbar's Number.

00:03:58.510 --> 00:04:01.000
And for humans, it's 150.

00:04:01.000 --> 00:04:03.650
That's an approximate
average of 150 people

00:04:03.650 --> 00:04:05.270
that your brain can have meaningful,

00:04:05.270 --> 00:04:07.770
simultaneous relationships with.

00:04:07.770 --> 00:04:10.460
This number pops up all
the time in the real world,

00:04:10.460 --> 00:04:12.780
from the size of early
agricultural villages

00:04:12.780 --> 00:04:15.400
to religious groups, to military units.

00:04:15.400 --> 00:04:17.960
But, why should maintaining
social relationships

00:04:17.960 --> 00:04:20.520
require so much brainpower?

00:04:20.520 --> 00:04:22.750
Well, it's deceptively complicated

00:04:22.750 --> 00:04:25.320
as any high school student can tell you.

00:04:25.320 --> 00:04:26.350
You don't just have to know

00:04:26.350 --> 00:04:28.770
how all these individuals feel about you,

00:04:28.770 --> 00:04:30.920
but how they all feel about each other.

00:04:30.920 --> 00:04:32.720
We're talking thousands and thousands

00:04:32.720 --> 00:04:35.810
of shifting relationships
with stacking states of mind.

00:04:35.810 --> 00:04:38.760
No wonder you need a supercomputer
to keep track of it all.

00:04:39.890 --> 00:04:41.630
Language, Dunbar claims,

00:04:41.630 --> 00:04:44.620
allowed us to streamline this process.

00:04:44.620 --> 00:04:47.660
We can compare stories about
other people's behavior.

00:04:47.660 --> 00:04:50.020
We can hear about events we
didn't personally witness.

00:04:50.020 --> 00:04:52.500
We can find out who
would be a reliable ally

00:04:52.500 --> 00:04:53.800
and who to avoid.

00:04:53.800 --> 00:04:55.750
And it's pretty hard to be a freeloader

00:04:55.750 --> 00:04:57.180
once word gets around.

00:04:57.180 --> 00:04:59.360
Some critics say that Dunbar's theory

00:04:59.360 --> 00:05:02.240
doesn't adequately explain
the specific process

00:05:02.240 --> 00:05:04.130
by which grooming turned into speech,

00:05:04.130 --> 00:05:07.240
and that language isn't enough
of an emotional substitute

00:05:07.240 --> 00:05:10.310
for the close and personal
nature of grooming.

00:05:10.310 --> 00:05:13.460
Still, there is persuasive
evidence for Dunbar's theory

00:05:13.460 --> 00:05:15.960
within the structure of grammar itself.

00:05:15.960 --> 00:05:17.860
One of the enduring mysteries of language

00:05:17.860 --> 00:05:21.030
is why grammar is so stunningly complex.

00:05:21.030 --> 00:05:21.863
Think about it.

00:05:21.863 --> 00:05:24.320
The same mental capacity that we use today

00:05:24.320 --> 00:05:27.260
to describe calculus and quantum mechanics

00:05:27.260 --> 00:05:31.540
evolved on the African Savanna
a hundred thousand years ago.

00:05:31.540 --> 00:05:33.260
What would prehistoric hunter-gatherers

00:05:33.260 --> 00:05:36.790
need all these tenses, and
clauses, and recursions for?

00:05:36.790 --> 00:05:39.377
You don't need much
grammatical flexibility to say,

00:05:39.377 --> 00:05:42.100
"There Buffalo," or
"Red berries make sick."

00:05:42.100 --> 00:05:43.897
You do need it, however, to say that,

00:05:43.897 --> 00:05:45.440
"Siobhan's angry at Lakshay

00:05:45.440 --> 00:05:47.650
for eating the banana that
she was saving for Sarah,

00:05:47.650 --> 00:05:50.340
even though he knew that
Sarah gave hers to Lily."

00:05:50.340 --> 00:05:52.500
Even the most simple
form of human grammar:

00:05:52.500 --> 00:05:56.050
subject, verb, object,
seems designed for gossip.

00:05:56.050 --> 00:05:58.200
Who did what to whom?

00:05:58.200 --> 00:06:00.010
Intentionality is baked in.

00:06:00.010 --> 00:06:02.370
Some think our whole
tradition of storytelling

00:06:02.370 --> 00:06:05.570
comes from this fundamental
narrative structure of language,

00:06:05.570 --> 00:06:07.650
which would mean that
everything from Shakespeare

00:06:07.650 --> 00:06:10.910
to "Squid Game" evolved
from tribal gossip.

00:06:10.910 --> 00:06:13.570
We just love hearing about
other people's problems,

00:06:13.570 --> 00:06:14.670
even if they're fictional.

00:06:14.670 --> 00:06:17.320
Today, gossip functions much the same way

00:06:17.320 --> 00:06:18.730
it did in the past.

00:06:18.730 --> 00:06:20.710
It's used to create social bonds,

00:06:20.710 --> 00:06:22.800
to stay informed about one's community,

00:06:22.800 --> 00:06:26.640
and to warn others to steer
clear of untrustworthy members.

00:06:26.640 --> 00:06:29.970
However, like anything that
delivers a dopamine hit,

00:06:29.970 --> 00:06:32.960
it can be overused or
exploited for profit.

00:06:32.960 --> 00:06:34.260
For a long time,

00:06:34.260 --> 00:06:36.630
tabloids were the primary
corporate suppliers

00:06:36.630 --> 00:06:38.080
of our gossip fix,

00:06:38.080 --> 00:06:39.940
but their turf has surely been taken over

00:06:39.940 --> 00:06:41.880
by social media companies.

00:06:41.880 --> 00:06:43.860
Apps like Twitter let us all get in

00:06:43.860 --> 00:06:46.040
on the global gossip frenzy,

00:06:46.040 --> 00:06:48.250
which raises real questions about the line

00:06:48.250 --> 00:06:50.740
between accountability and overkill.

00:06:50.740 --> 00:06:53.040
It's good that women in
the New York dating scene

00:06:53.040 --> 00:06:55.600
were warning each other
about West Elm Caleb,

00:06:55.600 --> 00:06:58.730
but does everyone on
earth need to weigh in?

00:06:58.730 --> 00:07:01.980
Though it can be used recklessly
and hurt innocent people.

00:07:01.980 --> 00:07:04.140
Gossip is far from the wicked vice

00:07:04.140 --> 00:07:06.290
that some moralizers make it out to be.

00:07:06.290 --> 00:07:09.900
Instead, it's a fundamental
part of our human nature,

00:07:09.900 --> 00:07:12.470
the tool that allowed us
to build larger communities

00:07:12.470 --> 00:07:15.430
and perhaps the origin of language itself.

00:07:15.430 --> 00:07:17.670
Turns out that dishing about other people

00:07:17.670 --> 00:07:19.670
is not the province of a small-minded,

00:07:19.670 --> 00:07:21.910
but the big-brained.

00:07:21.910 --> 00:07:23.770
One, two, three.

00:07:23.770 --> 00:07:25.800
Like the people...

00:07:25.800 --> 00:07:27.734
Does... (Dr. Erica breathing frantically)

00:07:27.734 --> 00:07:29.770
(Dr. Erica stuttering)

00:07:29.770 --> 00:07:32.233
That allowed us to build
larger communities.

00:07:33.310 --> 00:07:34.840
Why'd I say it like that?

00:07:34.840 --> 00:07:35.803
I'm too good!

